<!--
  Symmetry redesign: the three chains of a homotrimer share one sequence, and
  only the surface where they meet is redesigned.

      rosetta_link_scripts \
          -parser:protocol examples/symmetry_redesign.xml \
          -s examples/input_data/coiled_coil_trimer.pdb \
          -out:path output/symmetry_redesign

  The input is Lpp-56, the trimerisation domain of the Escherichia coli outer
  membrane lipoprotein: a parallel three stranded coiled coil of 50 residues
  per chain, from biological assembly 1 of PDB 1KFM.

  TiedPositions ties the three selectors position by position, so the first
  residue each one picks makes one group, the second the next, and so on. A
  group is held to a single amino acid, which is what makes the three chains
  come out identical. One selector per chain would instead say that a whole
  chain is one amino acid.

  interface_A, interface_B and interface_C pick the residues of one chain
  within 6 A of another chain: 23 of each chain here, 69 of the 150 in the
  trimer. On a coiled coil those are the a and d positions of the heptad, the
  core that holds the three helices together. design_selector names the three
  together, so everything outside that core keeps the sequence it came in
  with.

  The input has to be symmetric for this to be right, and that is why it is an
  assembly rather than a deposited model. 1KFM has one chain in the asymmetric
  unit, and the other two are made by the crystallographic three fold, so the
  three are exact copies and the selector picks the same positions on each.

  Measured on a trimer whose three chains were each deposited, the same kind
  of selector picked 14 residues on every chain, which passes the equal count
  check TiedPositions makes, but not the same 14: one chain had residues 1 and
  2 where another had 2 and 26. Tied position by position, that pairs residue
  1 of one chain with residue 2 of another. Equal counts are necessary, not
  sufficient, so check what a selector picks on each chain before tying
  selectors that were not built from exact symmetry.

  weight is left out, so each residue of a group carries one third, the value
  LigandMPNN uses for a homo-oligomer. temperature is over the LigandMPNN
  default of 0.1, which gives the two designs room to differ.

  Each chain of this input carries the same residue numbers, so homo_oligomer
  would tie the chains here as well. It ties every position of every chain
  rather than the ones a selector names, which says nothing about what is
  redesigned: design_selector decides that either way. What it does need is
  the repeated numbering, which a design coming out of RFdiffusion does not
  have. See examples/homo_oligomer_design.xml.

  LigandMPNN takes a single input pose whenever TiedPositions is set, since
  symmetry_residues has no _multi counterpart and one list covers a whole run.
  This stage is first in the protocol, so it receives one pose anyway.

  scRMSD_all pairs residue i of chain A with residue i of chain A, chain B
  with chain B and so on, so it says something only when the prediction names
  the chains the way the design does. A predictor has no reason to. On a
  homotrimer it can label them in the opposite cyclic order, which no rotation
  undoes, and the number then comes out large for a structure that matches.
  scRMSD_chain covers one chain against that same chain and is free of it.
  Read it for self consistency, and treat a large scRMSD_all on a
  homo-oligomer as a question rather than an answer.

  A helical bundle of this size is within reach of a prediction from the
  sequence by itself, which a larger domain is not: msa_mode is
  single_sequence here.
-->
<ROSETTASCRIPTS>

    <RESIDUE_SELECTORS>
        <Chain name="chain_A" chains="A" />
        <Chain name="chain_B" chains="B" />
        <Chain name="chain_C" chains="C" />

        <Not name="not_chain_A" selector="chain_A" />
        <Not name="not_chain_B" selector="chain_B" />
        <Not name="not_chain_C" selector="chain_C" />

        <Neighborhood name="facing_A" selector="not_chain_A"
                      distance="6.0" include_focus_in_subset="false" />
        <Neighborhood name="facing_B" selector="not_chain_B"
                      distance="6.0" include_focus_in_subset="false" />
        <Neighborhood name="facing_C" selector="not_chain_C"
                      distance="6.0" include_focus_in_subset="false" />

        <And name="interface_A" selectors="chain_A,facing_A" />
        <And name="interface_B" selectors="chain_B,facing_B" />
        <And name="interface_C" selectors="chain_C,facing_C" />

        <Or name="interface" selectors="interface_A,interface_B,interface_C" />
    </RESIDUE_SELECTORS>

    <MOVERS>
        <LigandMPNN name="design_sequence"
            batch_size="2"
            temperature="0.3"
            design_selector="interface"
            prefix_name="mpnn_"
            work_dir="01_ligandmpnn">
            <TiedPositions residues_selectors="interface_A,interface_B,interface_C" />
        </LigandMPNN>

        <ColabFold name="predict_structure"
            models="1,2"
            msa_mode="single_sequence"
            prefix_name="AF2_"
            replace_pose="true"
            work_dir="02_colabfold">
            <RMSD name="scRMSD_all" />
            <RMSD name="scRMSD_chain" calculation_selector="chain_A" />
            <RMSD name="scRMSD_interface" calculation_selector="interface" />
            <metric name="plddt_all" base_metric="pLDDT" />
            <metric name="plddt_interface" base_metric="pLDDT" selector="interface" />
            <metric name="pAE_all" base_metric="pAE" />
        </ColabFold>
    </MOVERS>

    <PROTOCOLS>
        <Add mover="design_sequence" />
        <Add mover="predict_structure" />
    </PROTOCOLS>

</ROSETTASCRIPTS>
